# CampusConnect – Project Rules

CampusConnect is a classroom management web app (similar to Google Classroom) built as an MCA final mini project. It must be fully working, clean, and professional.

## Structure
- Monorepo: /backend (Spring Boot) and /frontend (React).
- Backend: Java 21, Spring Boot 3, Maven, Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, Spring Security with JWT (access + refresh tokens), Bean Validation, Flyway, MySQL 8, springdoc-openapi, Lombok.
- Backend packages: com.campusconnect.{config, security, controller, service, repository, entity, dto, mapper, exception, util}.
- Use DTOs for all requests/responses; never expose entities directly.
- Use a global exception handler returning a consistent JSON error format: { timestamp, status, error, message, path }.
- Role-based access: TEACHER and STUDENT (plus ADMIN in phase 3). Enforce authorization in the service layer too (e.g., only the class owner can edit a class; only enrolled students can view it).
- Passwords hashed with BCrypt. Secrets and DB credentials come from application.yml with environment variable overrides.
- All schema changes via Flyway migrations (V1__init.sql, V2__..., etc.). Never use ddl-auto=update.
- Never edit a Flyway migration that has already been committed or applied. Put fixes and further schema changes in a new Vn migration. If a checksum mismatch appears after an exceptional rewrite, run `flyway repair` (or delete the bad row from `flyway_schema_history`) once, then restart.
- Paginate list endpoints.

- Frontend: React 18 + Vite + TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, React Router, TanStack Query, Axios (with an interceptor for JWT and token refresh), React Hook Form + Zod, Lucide icons.
- Frontend folders: src/{api, components/ui, components/layout, features/<feature>, hooks, lib, pages, routes, types}.

## Design rules (very important)
Visual reference: docs/design/CampusConnect-UI-Design.html. The UI must look natural and hand-designed, NOT AI-generated.
- The signature element is the notebook: every class is shown as a notebook cover in its class colour (stitched spine, elastic band, ribbon, folded corner, page edges, ruled paper label with a red margin line). The colour of a class must be consistent everywhere it appears (cards, banner, sidebar spines, planner tabs, to-do items).
- Fonts: Instrument Serif for page and section headings; Instrument Sans for all interface and body text; Caveat only where a person would write by hand (notebook labels and sticky notes). Nowhere else.
- One accent colour (ink blue) for actions, links, focus and selected states. Status colours: amber = due soon, brick = missing/errors, green = graded, blue = turned in.
- Subtle shading is allowed only for physical objects (notebook elastic, pencil, page edges). No decorative gradient backgrounds, no glassmorphism, no glowing effects, no emojis.
- Only one non-interactive animation: the notebooks dropping in on the login page. All other motion responds to user actions (hover lift on notebooks, focus rings, modals). Respect prefers-reduced-motion.
- Sentence case everywhere. Realistic Indian college content in seed data and empty states.
- Light and dark themes, both fully designed. Accessible: 4.5:1 text contrast, visible keyboard focus, labels on all inputs, aria-label on icon-only buttons.
- Class cover colours only: teal #0E6B66, maroon #86293F, forest #2F6A3B, indigo #363D8C, steel #2C5A84, ochre #8C5F0E.
- Spacing scale: 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32. Layout: top bar, left sidebar with cover spines, class banner as notebook, tabs (Stream, Classwork, Materials, People, Grades).
- Every list has a proper empty state, a loading skeleton, and an error state. Use relative dates and status labels in small coloured text. Fully responsive.

## Code quality
- Clean, readable, commented where useful. No dead code, no TODO placeholders, no mock data in production code paths.
- After finishing a task, make sure backend compiles and frontend builds with no TypeScript errors.
